#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f4b65e-b93a-51e8-b943-b3b53d47fffb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7784d8-f6a4-5e45-b03e-d76a27372c48" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111879 :tutorial/content "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." :tutorial/id #uuid "bcae8943-0768-5280-ad4d-a67f04877fbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2794 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
